{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "single_threshold",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "The parent uses one fixed rational softening profile in the effective temperature denominator, so for each fixed Delta the Arrhenius plot curvature versus T is controlled by a single crossover shape shared across all barrier values.",
      "child_relation": "Relax the assumption that disorder renormalization is governed by one universal crossover shape; let the barrier-softening efficiency itself vary with the thermal-to-barrier ratio in a second nonlinear way, producing different mid-range curvature for low-Delta and high-Delta conditions while preserving monotonic conductivity trends.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "T",
        "Delta"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "At fixed sigma0, k_B, and one shared disorder scale, intermediate-temperature conductivity rises can be either broader or more sharply accelerated depending on Delta/k_B T, creating a family of Arrhenius-curve bends that the parent's single crossover cannot match simultaneously."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "theta",
        "expression": "k_B*T/Delta",
        "role": "dimensionless thermal-to-barrier ratio controlling hopping activation regime"
      },
      {
        "name": "soft_parent",
        "expression": "T_dis*(Delta/(Delta + k_B*T))**2",
        "role": "current disorder-induced apparent barrier softening contribution"
      },
      {
        "name": "inv_slope_parent",
        "expression": "Delta/(k_B*(T + T_dis*(Delta/(Delta + k_B*T))**2))",
        "role": "current effective activation exponent determining conductivity curvature"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Most visible when k_B*T and Delta are comparable, i.e. theta ~ 0.2 to 3, so neither the low-T Arrhenius limit nor the high-T washed-out limit fully dominates; weak at Delta >> k_B*T or k_B*T >> Delta where both parent and child approach similar asymptotes.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "Within training, varying T at several fixed Delta values already exposes whether one shared crossover shape fits all curves. In holdout, the same mechanism extends to unsampled intermediate theta, where the child predicts systematic curvature redistribution rather than just a global rescaling.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent by sending the added shape-control correction to zero so the softening law reverts to the original T_dis*(Delta/(Delta + k_B*T))**2 form.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The current model assumes a single universal functional form for disorder-induced barrier softening across all thermal-to-barrier ratios, which is often too rigid for disordered hopping networks whose accessible-path statistics evolve with temperature.",
    "baseline_assumption": "A single effective activation scale represents the disorder landscape, and its temperature renormalization follows one fixed rational crossover shape for all sampled T and Delta.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Temperature changes which subsets of localized hopping paths percolate, so the apparent barrier reduction can change curvature with thermal activation fraction rather than following one universal squared ratio law.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "T",
      "Delta",
      "T_dis",
      "k_B"
    ],
    "rationale": "The parent target mapping is conductivity as a monotone function of T and Delta through one effective exponent, with disorder entering only through a single shared softening shape. Here T and Delta vary independently while carrier density, geometry, and disorder statistics remain fixed. A realistic next refinement is to relax that fixed-shape assumption, not to add a new transport channel. The observable target change is in the T-Delta conductivity surface itself: for two different Delta values, the parent with one refitted parameter set can shift the crossover position and overall strength only in the restricted way allowed by its single rational form, but it cannot independently broaden one curve's mid-T rise while keeping another curve's crossover sharper when both lie in the comparable-energy regime k_B T ~ Delta. This is a genuine change in the scored mapping, not just an auxiliary reinterpretation. The effect is scientifically admissible in disordered hopping because the subset of energetically viable paths changes with thermal energy. The refinement should remain monotone so increasing Delta still lowers sigma and increasing T still raises the baseline hopping conductivity. Limitations: if all sampled data lie deep in asymptotic regimes, the added curvature freedom may be hard to distinguish, so the contrast relies on having temperatures that probe the crossover region."
  },
